# Asset-defined radial-menu tool presentation

Source proposal: [ToolHeadPresentation.proposal.md](ToolHeadPresentation.proposal.md).
Status: complete. Implementation and authored patches have fresh technical validation, user-reported visual acceptance, second review, and independent stage-completion audit.

## Goal and constraints

Display readable, deliberately oriented tool heads using asset-defined radial-menu transforms. Handles may extend outside the wedge and be clipped intentionally. Configured presentation replaces the inventory GUI transform and category head offset for supported radial-menu draws.

The proposal governs this plan. Preserve ordinary inventory rendering, candidate selection, ordering, equipment operations, input, and hit testing. Non-tool entries, tool-mode artwork, and center labels retain their existing presentation. Automatic bounds calculation, geometry retention, containment searches, and automatic rotation/shrinking are outside scope.

Follow repository code practices: documented methods/classes, comments for nontrivial logic, visibility-first regions, focused files, directional dependencies, and thin composition roots. Run all build and test tools through subagents. User-run visual acceptance remains separate from source and headless evidence; this plan does not authorize launching the game.

## Open decisions

The schema, matrix convention, initial coverage, and ordinary-mesh support are retained. The source proposal's Dedicated renderer and support section replaces the original hook boundary with public engine preparation and mesh submission. The shader/state contract and Selected state and post-preparation policy now define exact enclosing-state restoration and explicit cancellation after invalidating callbacks. Preserve single preparation; cancellation consumes the invocation, reports once per collectible, and reevaluates eligibility on the next invocation. Revalidate reusable engine behavior against versions other than the inspected 1.22.7.0. User-reported visual acceptance requires no further numeric artwork tuning.

## Phase 1: Establish asset and rendering contracts

Outcome: implementation and patch authors have a verified integration point and one documented presentation convention.

Historical completion applies to the original hook-based integration decision. Asset/schema/coverage findings remain reusable; the hook boundary is superseded by the approved dedicated renderer. Its replacement contracts and verification are now Phase 3 work.

- [x] Inspect relevant repository instructions, asset patch conventions, existing transform types, `QuickToolItemIcon`, and the radial-menu rendering pipeline.
- [x] Inspect current base-game item rendering to identify how mesh/texture selection, callbacks, GUI transforms, lighting, and depth are applied.
  - [x] Select a scoped override boundary that replaces GUI presentation while preserving applicable item-specific preparation and a single callback invocation.
  - [x] Define renderer eligibility and safe fallback when the override is unavailable or a custom renderer cannot honor it.
- [x] Resolve and document the asset schema in the proposal.
  - [x] Define model-space rotation in degrees, positive uniform scale, model-space translation, and optional screen-space wedge-relative rotation.
  - [x] Define defaults independent of inventory GUI transforms, a consistent pivot, transform order, reference scale, and the meaning of omitted wedge rotation.
  - [x] Specify placement relative to the wedge icon position and how menu size/hover apply once; prohibit fixed screen coordinates in asset values.
  - [x] Reuse the engine transform data model where suitable and document any concrete gap requiring additional fields.
- [x] Inventory the quick-swap tool families and representative model/material variants; record intended shared patches and necessary model-specific overrides in this plan.
- [x] Assign ownership: assets provide settings, a focused item-rendering component resolves/applies them, and the menu supplies wedge context and existing capture/halo/clipping.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Schema and integration decisions have current source or installed-engine evidence and are recorded before dependent code or patches are authored.
- [x] Coverage and unsupported-renderer boundaries are explicit; no decision requires changing inventory policy or shared GUI transforms.

### Initial asset coverage

Inspected the installed survival item definitions on 2026-10-06: all 23 files with root `tool-*` or `weapon-*` tags are under `game:itemtypes/tool/`. These tags feed `QuickToolCandidateCache.DiscoverSlot`; production grouping is dynamic and includes combined tags, not just the legacy `EnumTool` table. The inventory below is the initial patch scope. Paths are relative to that asset directory; material variants can share a transform only when their selected geometry and prepared model behavior agree.

| Asset file(s) | Shared patch groups and model-specific overrides to author |
| --- | --- |
| `pickaxe.json` | Separate base and iron/meteoriciron/steel shapes; represent copper, bronze, and iron/steel. |
| `prospectingpick.json` | Own head framing; share material variants of the same prospecting-pick geometry. Its combined pickaxe/prospecting tags do not justify reusing pickaxe framing. |
| `axe-stone.json`, `axe-metal.json` | Separate stone, scrap, copper/bronze felling, iron-shaped felling (including gold/silver), and each ruined head type: bearded, battle, bardiche, double. |
| `shovel.json`, `hoe.json` | Separate stone and metal head geometry in each file; represent flint, copper/bronze, iron/steel. |
| `hammer.json`, `scythe.json`, `saw.json`, `chisel.json`, `shears.json`, `cleaver.json`, `solderingiron.json` | Separate transform per file/model; share compatible material variants. Shears/cleaver must retain their complete resolved tag groups. |
| `knife.json` | Separate stone/bone, standard metal, and ruined dagger/stiletto/khanjar/baselard shapes; verify any type sharing against resolved geometry. |
| `blade.json` | Separate falx, blackguard, longsword, forlorn, scrap, gladius, arming, claymore, sabre, corresponding ruined shapes, and admin longsword; share texture-only metals within each actual shape. |
| `spear.json` | Separate stone, metal shape variants, scrap, hacking, ornate gold/silver, erel projectile-shaped spear, and ruined boar/voulge/fork/ranseur heads. |
| `club.json` | Separate generic wooden, scrap, scrapmace, and ruined flanged/morningstar/spiked/warhammer heads; `weapon-melee` alone still qualifies in current discovery. |
| `bow.json` | Separate simple/crude/long/recurve geometry; check callback-selected/alternate meshes before sharing. Frame the recognisable working section rather than inventing a tool head. |
| `sling.json` | Own working-section framing; check folded and charge variants only where selectable by GUI preparation. |
| `wrench.json`, `crowbar.json` | Separate actual shape families: wrench model groups and copper versus iron crowbar. Share material variants only after shape comparison. |
| `tongs.json`, `tongsmetal.json` | Separate wooden and metal models; verify narrow/medium/wide callback-selected openings remain readable with the selected file's transform. If one transform cannot cover the opening range, retain fallback for that asset until a concrete extension is designed. |

Shields/round shields have no root category tags in the inspected assets and are not initial patch targets merely because the legacy enum table includes Shield. Untagged tools, standalone tool heads, ammunition, mod-added definitions, block items, and non-tool entries (including this mod's healing-item patches and the virtual light entry) retain existing presentation. Do not add discovery tags to expand coverage. Unknown or registered custom GUI renderers retain fallback regardless of asset metadata.

